    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="what">What counts as a digital product — and what actually sells</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">A digital product is anything you make once and sell repeatedly as a file — a template, a planner, a guide, a workbook, a preset. What sells is not the format; it's how clearly it solves one specific problem for one specific person.</p>
    <p class="reveal">The trap is thinking the format is the product. It isn't. Nobody buys "a PDF." They buy the hour it saves them, the decision it makes for them, the mess it cleans up. <strong>The most ordinary format solving a sharp, real problem will outsell a beautiful thing that solves a vague one.</strong></p>
    <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>A quick test for whether an idea will sell: can you name, in one sentence, the exact moment someone reaches for it? "The planner you open on Sunday night when the week already feels behind." If you can't picture the moment, the product isn't sharp enough yet.</p></div>

    <div class="triptych offset reveal">
