      <div class="veil"></div>
      <div class="inner"><p>Ship the modest one. The market will tell you what the good one is.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> Common Mistake</span><p>Building for six weeks in private, then launching to silence. You didn't learn anything in those six weeks except how to hide. One rough product in front of one real person beats a flawless one no one has seen.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="offer">The offer comes before the product</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Design the offer — the specific outcome, for a specific person, at a specific price — before you build the file. Building first is why beautiful digital products sit unsold.</p>
    <p class="reveal">An offer is not a file. An offer is a promise. "A 30-page template" is a file. "The system that plans your entire content month in one sitting" is an offer — same file, completely different thing to buy.</p>

    <div class="detail reveal">
      <div class="photo mask img" style="background-image:url(/images/library/detail-hands-writing-journal.jpg);background-size:cover;background-position:center"></div>
      <div class="say"><div class="k">The line that sells</div><p>Write the promise before you make the product. If you can't finish the sentence "this gets you from ___ to ___," you're not ready to build — you're ready to think.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> Your offer is ready when</span><ul>
      <li>You can name the exact outcome in one sentence</li>
      <li>You can picture the exact woman who needs it</li>
      <li>You've chosen the lightest format that delivers it</li>
      <li>You've set a price you can say out loud without flinching</li>
    </ul></div>
